#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133607 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "19ae0632-68ac-5da9-8adf-cf71d5ecbc7c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the QR Factorization of a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "f042812b-fb14-520a-bb6d-08631d941d04" :question/math-academy-id "e-5586" :question/problem "![](images/8d/8d29d0f5dd5e0d2c79c8d2c40c49b358a01dc5bb5536153fe05068773ef1e228.svg)\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns $\\mathbf{q}_1,\\mathbf{q}_2$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of $q_{11} \\cdot r_{12}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ![](images/71/714ad80d47ea58159e0e486f5bfc5a6657fd441bc47aa2b8fa7230a89bdb0445.svg)\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{12}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} & 2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/a6/a6377dfb1e7ed7afafe348354dd21b2195c12495d9a5a6dcd98fec3aaeb8162d.svg)\n\nFinally, $q_{11} \\cdot r_{12} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\right) \\cdot \\left({2\\sqrt6}\\right) = 2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133633 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd18abf3-b551-55d6-a573-690745a65b3e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given an Orthogonal Basis in Terms of the Columns of a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "c6fe3751-c00b-592b-903e-567d95174cd2" :question/math-academy-id "e-5959" :question/problem "![](images/db/db19bfb8029513dcb3dc109a0752f87ecf405a6aa56881849f553d30fa1085ff.svg)\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above, where $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2,\\mathbf{v}_3 \\big\\}$ is an orthogonal set of vectors. You're given that $\\Vert\\mathbf{v}_1\\Vert =2,$ $\\Vert\\mathbf{v}_2\\Vert =3,$ $\\Vert\\mathbf{v}_3\\Vert =4,$ and that the system of equations below describes the relationship between the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}={a}_{2}-{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3}={a}_{3}-3{v}_{1}+2{v}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $r_{13}+r_{23}+r_{33}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we normalize the vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2,\\mathbf{v}_3 \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{2}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{1}=2{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{3}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{2}=3{q}_{2} \\\\ {q}_{3} & =\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{3}}{4}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{3}=4{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the set $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{q}_1,\\mathbf{q}_2,\\mathbf{q}_3 \\big\\}$ will be both orthogonal and normalized.\n\nNext, let's rewrite the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3$ as a linear combination of the vectors $\\mathbf{q}_1, \\mathbf{q}_2, \\mathbf{q}_3 \\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/f5/f53a5eb3b2d24ad186382f8eed42775b2ee61d34a20a0af821a38c17d846e873.svg)\n\nThe final system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n![](images/27/2797cd9e375b3a28f3f8afc8c49b85e202ad5cc87fb5a332ebb9abd73f388ee3.svg)\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nr_{13}+r_{23}+r_{33} = 6+(-6)+4 = 4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133678 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133862 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38edbd80-2aef-5b3e-8dd9-de9f2fbeca9b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Triangular Factor of a QR Factorization Given the Orthogonal Factor"}] :question/id #uuid "0a812585-d467-562d-acda-e379ac9dc544" :question/math-academy-id "e-8324" :question/problem "![](images/db/db0726bd1986ffe1876c4b5a93b7b14b4f4c1496dc6b28ebd6ac002ea878cde2.svg)\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Q$ shown above. Find the matrix $R$ such that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ has orthonormal columns, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by $Q^T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{T}\\cdot A & ={Q}^{T}\\cdot QR \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & =({Q}^{T}\\,Q)R \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & ={I}_{2}R \\\\ R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that $Q^TQ = I_2$ since the columns of $Q$ are orthonormal.\n\nNow, we calculate the matrix $R\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133679 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133863 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ed5119c-5ce8-52dc-bd57-f9439ea199e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given a Matrix With Orthogonal Columns"}] :question/id #uuid "155ed7f9-909e-5550-b1ef-f707b49f7fde" :question/math-academy-id "e-8325" :question/problem "![](images/12/128c3de38ca9c80e3e0d22d939fe05c88404fed97dee1a94f59aff5331f56172.svg)\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns $\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns $\\mathbf{q}_1,\\mathbf{q}_2$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of $q_{12} \\cdot r_{22}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct a matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2\\mathbf{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by $Q^T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/5d/5d8b0271c6dcd970ec7c8d652433a798daf97e67792c47db1f6eef4bd39ad651.svg)\n\nFinally, $q_{12} \\cdot r_{22} = -\\dfrac{2}{3} \\cdot 3 = -2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133680 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133864 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1304189f-61ac-53c5-9b09-8e365f763136" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Dimensions of Factors in a QR Factorization"}] :question/id #uuid "35fdd570-1c80-5b0d-9c5f-fa277bd30afb" :question/math-academy-id "e-8326" :question/problem "Given that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of a $3 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $Q$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix\n\nII. The columns of $Q$ form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$\n\nIII. $R$ is an upper triangular matrix" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of an $m \\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n$$\n\\underset{{\\large m} \\times {\\large n}}{A}\n=\n\\underset{{\\large m} \\times {\\large n}}{Q} \\times \\underset{{\\large n} \\times {\\large n}}{R},\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $Q$ is an $m \\times n$ matrix whose columns form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$\n\n- $R$ is an $n \\times n$ upper triangular matrix\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. $Q$ should be a $3\\times 2$ matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the columns of $Q$ form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $R$ must be a $2 \\times 2$ upper triangular matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363203 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133864 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1304189f-61ac-53c5-9b09-8e365f763136" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Dimensions of Factors in a QR Factorization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22a267aa-19c7-5a4e-b20a-596b46361ff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363205} {:answer/id #uuid "9214fe6d-7921-5395-b98b-78b02b3eb40e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363206} {:answer/id #uuid "acb9ea7d-cb59-5ac8-99fb-485c266d2c73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363207} {:answer/id #uuid "12ee20c8-1283-5d6e-86b4-f79be9b91d2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363208} {:answer/id #uuid "e288ffeb-c37d-5dfb-8914-81566c61db71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9214fe6d-7921-5395-b98b-78b02b3eb40e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363206} :answer-field/id #uuid "58b0761b-7cd9-5618-bf38-706cbf7f45cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4ea55d3c-3433-59ef-97d4-45fcd98ab0e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-140896" :question/problem "Given that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of a $5\\times 4$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The columns of $Q$ are orthogonal\n\nII. $Q$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix\n\nIII. $R$ is a  square  matrix" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=QR$ is a  QR factorization of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n$$\n{A}_{m\\times n}={Q}_{m\\times n}\\times {R}_{n\\times n},\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $Q$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix whose columns form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$\n\n- $R$ is an $n\\times n$ upper triangular matrix\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since the columns of $Q$ form an orthonormal basis, they are orthogonal.\n\n- Statement II is false. $Q$ should be a $5\\times 4$  matrix.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $R$ must be a $4\\times 4$ upper triangular matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363217 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd18abf3-b551-55d6-a573-690745a65b3e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given an Orthogonal Basis in Terms of the Columns of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce45e94d-baf5-5098-ac2d-d794c6429bb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363219} {:answer/id #uuid "448670c4-632d-5013-a236-b59dc6cbcc16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363220} {:answer/id #uuid "cb4bc888-dff7-59f0-a773-62a45b1023fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363221} {:answer/id #uuid "ccaa65a2-e9e6-5cda-b206-709db184a16e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363222} {:answer/id #uuid "c20f970a-bd81-5297-9fcb-5e40ddbce489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb4bc888-dff7-59f0-a773-62a45b1023fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363221} :answer-field/id #uuid "4547ff35-0e1d-5d8f-b076-6bc2886e7c0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d67166f-3bff-552c-8a7c-19cfbf3fd7f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-140900" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. You're given that the system of equations given below connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{2}+{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{3}+2{q}_{1}-{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{13}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{23}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{2}+{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{3}+2{q}_{1}-{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}=4{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}=-2{q}_{1}+2{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3}=-6{q}_{1}+3{q}_{2}+3{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, this system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{13}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{23}=-6+2+3=-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432334 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd18abf3-b551-55d6-a573-690745a65b3e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given an Orthogonal Basis in Terms of the Columns of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03f77c16-15f2-5876-91ea-1f42c2f49146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432273} {:answer/id #uuid "040cb4ad-6adb-5b45-a356-8c24b8b87c5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432274} {:answer/id #uuid "589b7138-e564-53cf-b058-e2dd1efd855b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432290} {:answer/id #uuid "6d46eee3-4e02-5798-955c-810b16c0b207" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432297} {:answer/id #uuid "e88e24de-26c6-5e79-8da5-47c1a175c8d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432312}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "040cb4ad-6adb-5b45-a356-8c24b8b87c5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432274} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed11c1c1-5852-58e2-a3f1-e49b2bb10b4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432325}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e5f7e59e-edf4-549f-aea0-27ed8989142e" :question/math-academy-id "q-140901" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ \\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖} & \\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖} & \\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above, where $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is an orthogonal set of vectors. You're given that $‖{v}_{1}‖=1,$ $‖{v}_{2}‖=2,$ $‖{v}_{3}‖=3,$ and that the system of equations below describes the relationship between the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$ \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}={a}_{2}-{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3}={a}_{3}-2{v}_{2}+{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{33}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we normalize the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{1}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{1}={q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{2}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{2}=2{q}_{2} \\\\ {q}_{3} & =\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{3}}{3}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{3}=3{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the set $\\{{q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}\\}$ will be both orthogonal and normalized.\n\nNext, let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}={a}_{2}-{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3}={a}_{3}-2{v}_{2}+{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}={v}_{1}={q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}={v}_{1}+{v}_{2}={q}_{1}+2{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3}=-{v}_{1}+2{v}_{2}+{v}_{3}=-{q}_{1}+4{q}_{2}+3{q}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{33}=1+(-1)+3=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133863 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ed5119c-5ce8-52dc-bd57-f9439ea199e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given a Matrix With Orthogonal Columns"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a027607c-b49e-5d51-8e9f-696efe340642" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363226} {:answer/id #uuid "66dfc0c2-4c41-5b5c-8ca9-8963c9494355" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363227} {:answer/id #uuid "92170d95-d6d3-5882-96d9-9f06a90067ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363228} {:answer/id #uuid "8f8b6e55-0d08-5b04-9194-57e762fe0902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363229} {:answer/id #uuid "25a800e8-0c9b-5cbf-9949-6d4a63eb0a1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66dfc0c2-4c41-5b5c-8ca9-8963c9494355" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363227} :answer-field/id #uuid "48f82e91-6709-5adc-99bd-29df5cbf4521" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "836a6cda-4c5e-5dc1-9726-f5d184348fd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-140902" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{22}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{22}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388792 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "19ae0632-68ac-5da9-8adf-cf71d5ecbc7c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the QR Factorization of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35450620-e1c1-5553-8d89-babffec49fa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388794} {:answer/id #uuid "9509bf92-21de-58cf-a928-febc07388708" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388795} {:answer/id #uuid "eba19a2d-d967-5a67-9c11-60ad3d49f2c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388796} {:answer/id #uuid "ab55f8c6-2adf-5f61-aac6-7bf5271da29d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388797} {:answer/id #uuid "2fe3e312-3a15-5ca5-b692-01ea036db64f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fe3e312-3a15-5ca5-b692-01ea036db64f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388798} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bf69525-3508-5fe7-99c0-fbb77383cf15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2fb99b60-4344-5bc1-af82-6f45b3524b97" :question/math-academy-id "q-140911" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{31}\\cdot {r}_{12}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-(\\frac{4}{2})[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{31}\\cdot {r}_{12}=(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2})=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392389 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133862 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38edbd80-2aef-5b3e-8dd9-de9f2fbeca9b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Triangular Factor of a QR Factorization Given the Orthogonal Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4d46fe7-9f20-5438-8833-4e642aec5804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3} & -\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392391} {:answer/id #uuid "b70257ab-9b65-525e-91bc-ee86b6bb7261" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392392} {:answer/id #uuid "ec10b933-18cd-56bc-9910-9c2d3d3d750b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3} & \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392393} {:answer/id #uuid "cd7ff5bf-9015-5fce-97ea-fc8f5bdf576d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392394} {:answer/id #uuid "2675dc3f-685d-5210-a1df-c1d57411573c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd7ff5bf-9015-5fce-97ea-fc8f5bdf576d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392394} :answer-field/id #uuid "41b85c88-2a2e-56d0-81b5-85750fc56b04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1cc11afa-a8f2-5bea-a1c2-faf40b2b0365" :question/math-academy-id "q-40836" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Q$ shown above. Find the matrix $R$ such that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ has orthonormal columns, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{T}\\cdot A & ={Q}^{T}\\cdot QR \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & =({Q}^{T}\\,Q)R \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & ={I}_{2}R \\\\ R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that ${Q}^{T}Q={I}_{2}$ since the columns of $Q$ are orthonormal.\n\nNow, we calculate the matrix $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134324 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "19ae0632-68ac-5da9-8adf-cf71d5ecbc7c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the QR Factorization of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fbcea61a-f7c5-5e90-95bf-e30e97fb1c5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392397} {:answer/id #uuid "87a540ed-333f-527d-82f8-aa109a53d564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392398} {:answer/id #uuid "10193a27-2949-56aa-bfb7-aac130ca8727" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392399} {:answer/id #uuid "2ce7f102-17d8-5df4-8505-ab11eafbcb85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392400} {:answer/id #uuid "5f83a4ce-78d8-5078-bb57-e6fcb33b7384" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10193a27-2949-56aa-bfb7-aac130ca8727" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392399} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac78dc7d-8434-5df2-894a-caad12e5602a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392396}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "77f48d85-1f2c-59c8-b53a-85db3bc9d5a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-40838" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{12}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]-(-\\frac{12}{6})[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} & -2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} & -2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{12}=(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})\\cdot (-2\\sqrt{6})=2\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134326 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "19ae0632-68ac-5da9-8adf-cf71d5ecbc7c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the QR Factorization of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "145087dc-06f8-558a-b8f8-2208b84355c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364160} {:answer/id #uuid "ff44fb5d-5784-50a2-8670-9d33e2a5aacc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364161} {:answer/id #uuid "b69119fb-822b-5880-80ae-95eff4fed6cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364162} {:answer/id #uuid "2e7ca4a4-511e-50ee-bf48-b7280e7b04b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364163} {:answer/id #uuid "6986252e-a34a-52a9-8eb9-deea8952c9f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e7ca4a4-511e-50ee-bf48-b7280e7b04b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364163} :answer-field/id #uuid "14335add-728c-5e2f-bb76-9b14164c8f1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364159}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ba99e80b-ab29-52f3-91a8-3caa3a6f75d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-40845" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{12}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**. We We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-(-\\frac{9}{9})[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{12}=(-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}})\\cdot (-3)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134352 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd18abf3-b551-55d6-a573-690745a65b3e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given an Orthogonal Basis in Terms of the Columns of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f68f4b1-032c-586b-a36d-fc805b96dfee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379824} {:answer/id #uuid "98233510-2aa1-59df-8104-73b9ce088a12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379825} {:answer/id #uuid "aaf28906-45b4-583d-9cb2-7ecf5f6587a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379826} {:answer/id #uuid "ea1a2d8d-138a-5868-84f1-904300ef6336" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379827} {:answer/id #uuid "bc4bf897-5a8d-5780-adc6-fd6269527019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98233510-2aa1-59df-8104-73b9ce088a12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379825} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3588995-8c6c-57f9-9ecb-afb3ad54a3b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "772d3a4c-3877-5b96-a25a-b75eff00d425" :question/math-academy-id "q-41415" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. You're given that the system of equations given below connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{2}+2{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{6}{a}_{3}-4{q}_{1}+{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{12}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{23}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{2}+2{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{6}{a}_{3}-4{q}_{1}+{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}=2{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}=-8{q}_{1}+4{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3}=24{q}_{1}-6{q}_{2}+6{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, this system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -8 & 24 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{12}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{23}=-8+4+(-6)=-10.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134356 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133863 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ed5119c-5ce8-52dc-bd57-f9439ea199e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given a Matrix With Orthogonal Columns"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3242e45-8dfe-5fe6-82af-0059d6bebf2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132176} {:answer/id #uuid "c3a90b7c-3d63-53f3-854c-41377ac17b2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132177} {:answer/id #uuid "cb68c06b-ba5a-5c65-98ed-312912d7168d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132178} {:answer/id #uuid "1c47712f-eea4-599f-b333-6824af8370c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132179} {:answer/id #uuid "54f799fc-07e7-5466-9e13-83b88c2a6351" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132180}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c47712f-eea4-599f-b333-6824af8370c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132179} :answer-field/id #uuid "e90bc570-39fe-5864-8811-4ee9e80d5e1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132175}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fad5b7f6-f745-5d1b-8800-91f91ba131b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-41419" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{12}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} & 0\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{12}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot 0=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432329 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "19ae0632-68ac-5da9-8adf-cf71d5ecbc7c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the QR Factorization of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39f67c12-f06d-5443-9c63-0e245edcb8a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432281} {:answer/id #uuid "6baa4982-1a24-5642-9737-70e53742cc5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432296} {:answer/id #uuid "8dbb6cf7-0fa9-5dca-bc3c-da7fadfea2d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432301} {:answer/id #uuid "a86a94a2-9208-5170-9e77-a036ff3be9fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432305} {:answer/id #uuid "d63bd3dc-77da-5863-8a02-ddc20391b233" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8dbb6cf7-0fa9-5dca-bc3c-da7fadfea2d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432301} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ebc4500-ebb6-58bb-9b74-0082d64a7b1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8825d6b3-93be-5a03-9cc7-5000a40034aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-41421" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{11}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-(-\\frac{9}{9})[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 6\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 6\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{11}=(\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}})\\cdot (3)=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}=2\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432326 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd18abf3-b551-55d6-a573-690745a65b3e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given an Orthogonal Basis in Terms of the Columns of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "003af35e-f600-5607-8b84-03a3fd662edc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432272} {:answer/id #uuid "061380b4-8fa4-5c94-aa1d-0aa7eefd118f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432275} {:answer/id #uuid "49b4d1d7-f1c4-5298-a995-47dd049adbd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432287} {:answer/id #uuid "70fe47a0-cdd1-5db7-baec-800ac5639ef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432298} {:answer/id #uuid "cfb5e98f-f826-54b9-8c31-84bb424125fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432310}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cfb5e98f-f826-54b9-8c31-84bb424125fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432310} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcc7880c-e1d9-51f3-9d82-55e1eee95989" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432324}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3a08d141-4335-56a2-8562-7ce4a6f3683b" :question/math-academy-id "q-41484" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. You're given that the system of equations given below connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{5}{a}_{2}+6{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{3}-2{q}_{1}+{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{23}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{5}{a}_{2}+6{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{3}-2{q}_{1}+{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}=3{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}=-30{q}_{1}+5{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3}=8{q}_{1}-4{q}_{2}+4{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, this system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -30 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{23}=3+8+(-4)=7.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364178 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd18abf3-b551-55d6-a573-690745a65b3e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given an Orthogonal Basis in Terms of the Columns of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74849d77-3f75-57fb-9eda-c7eb146ef089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364180} {:answer/id #uuid "ae17bbad-6406-590f-9037-515c2d2e3302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364181} {:answer/id #uuid "80193887-56ef-5f55-9899-81a4ca2a5bfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364182} {:answer/id #uuid "d9ed3821-6db7-500c-afa6-af890beb1af8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364183} {:answer/id #uuid "ee368a24-e35e-5530-bc4c-c0b11699a84f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80193887-56ef-5f55-9899-81a4ca2a5bfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364182} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfd9129a-1c55-5b4e-92c4-8f9a2d89199f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0711fb88-caa5-5f45-81df-dbacaf0d183c" :question/math-academy-id "q-41486" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. You're given that the system of equations given below connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{2}+8{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{3}-3{q}_{1}+2{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{13}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{33}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{2}+8{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{3}-3{q}_{1}+2{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}=6{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}=-16{q}_{1}+2{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3}=9{q}_{1}-6{q}_{2}+3{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, this system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}6 & -16 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{13}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{33}=9+2+3=14.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364571 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133864 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1304189f-61ac-53c5-9b09-8e365f763136" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Dimensions of Factors in a QR Factorization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8dbe5dc9-2c40-591a-8dc9-2a6047634590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364573} {:answer/id #uuid "03859ee3-4c04-5da9-ac3f-9f5b14b65a8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364574} {:answer/id #uuid "8f52a007-3c47-5c22-8911-2baaae52e9c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364575} {:answer/id #uuid "01eba2d3-fc91-5570-a105-b568905a237c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364576} {:answer/id #uuid "3901f3b0-6b91-5ac4-98c8-d16a45cdb47d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8dbe5dc9-2c40-591a-8dc9-2a6047634590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364573} :answer-field/id #uuid "14c06a9a-d5bd-588b-bc25-ab1de6fdd166" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364572}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "14a18e20-8a94-54a8-afff-0a503b7d967c" :question/math-academy-id "q-95916" :question/problem "Given that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The columns of $Q$ are orthonormal\n\nII. $Q$ is a square matrix\n\nIII. $R$ is a square matrix" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n$$\n{A}_{m\\times n}={Q}_{m\\times n}\\times {R}_{n\\times n},\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $Q$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix whose columns form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$\n\n- $R$ is an $n\\times n$ upper triangular matrix\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since the columns of $Q$ form an orthonormal basis, they are orthonormal.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $Q$ must be a $2\\times 2$ matrix, it is square.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $R$ must be a $2\\times 2$ matrix, it is square.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432327 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133864 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1304189f-61ac-53c5-9b09-8e365f763136" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Dimensions of Factors in a QR Factorization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0855b0c5-d3cb-5d5a-87d3-6ae2573808c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432276} {:answer/id #uuid "40511cf5-4e38-545c-92bd-6f12dbd5bbe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432283} {:answer/id #uuid "429b9536-a311-5367-b367-ede0065e5d2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432284} {:answer/id #uuid "60ddcb4a-b5db-50a3-82d4-5c076834cb99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432293} {:answer/id #uuid "7bc9001c-a8a7-5655-ad38-e28393932a25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bc9001c-a8a7-5655-ad38-e28393932a25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432299} :answer-field/id #uuid "77a7384a-2954-50d8-86d9-02fc2c11bedd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432321}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b19eecb-3ecc-5584-a617-4bf8675e0b01" :question/math-academy-id "q-95917" :question/problem "Given that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of a $4\\times 2$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $Q$ is a $4\\times 2$ upper triangular matrix\n\nII. The columns of $Q$ are unit vectors\n\nIII. $R$ is a $4\\times 2$ matrix" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n$$\n{A}_{m\\times n}={Q}_{m\\times n}\\times {R}_{n\\times n},\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $Q$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix whose columns form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$\n\n- $R$ is an $n\\times n$ upper triangular matrix\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. $Q$ should be a $4\\times 2$ matrix, but is not necessarily upper triangular.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the columns of $Q$ form an orthonormal basis, they are unit vectors.\n\n- Statement III is false. $R$ must be a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432328 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133864 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1304189f-61ac-53c5-9b09-8e365f763136" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Dimensions of Factors in a QR Factorization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1194f3e3-1844-5ab9-9daf-5533b0a96a9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432277} {:answer/id #uuid "30cd0132-8138-5a90-bf6d-84623e148b9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432278} {:answer/id #uuid "3108f868-6810-5414-b79d-ed9c59cf14cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432279} {:answer/id #uuid "62bcf903-0255-59aa-affe-9d6ade55eb53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432294} {:answer/id #uuid "eede4f00-fcca-5952-bf0b-1b45f39cc595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432314}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3108f868-6810-5414-b79d-ed9c59cf14cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432279} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fd0a920-47da-5738-85dc-fcfde1071d1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432323}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "71004f41-ae2f-50fd-9fdc-88ec92c18337" :question/math-academy-id "q-95919" :question/problem "Given that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of a $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $Q$ is a square matrix\n\nII. $\\text{Col}(A)$ is spanned by the columns of $Q$\n\nIII. $R$ is a square matrix" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n$$\n{A}_{m\\times n}={Q}_{m\\times n}\\times {R}_{n\\times n},\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $Q$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix whose columns form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$\n\n- $R$ is an $n\\times n$ upper triangular matrix\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. $Q$ should be an $m\\times n$ matrix, so $Q$ is not necessarily a square matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true.  Since the columns of $Q$ form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A),$ they must span $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $R$ must be an $m\\times n$ matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432331 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133864 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1304189f-61ac-53c5-9b09-8e365f763136" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Dimensions of Factors in a QR Factorization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34dee133-5da2-587d-bf0e-88da035eca52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432280} {:answer/id #uuid "b3c6518b-cfa4-5881-b743-5099d5ac42c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432307} {:answer/id #uuid "c4b65933-5b0f-5c15-a729-3b530d4943b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432309} {:answer/id #uuid "ee9fd3db-d080-510c-8cba-0c10cffe8599" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432313} {:answer/id #uuid "f0b12155-382a-5236-96d0-5241fdc65d8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432315}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee9fd3db-d080-510c-8cba-0c10cffe8599" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432313} :answer-field/id #uuid "16d9df2f-7dae-571c-9898-8b25f88afb03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a6266ee7-76af-56d5-9957-fa402398ddd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-95923" :question/problem "Given that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of an $8\\times 4$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $Q$ is an $8\\times 4$ matrix\n\nII. $R$ is an $8\\times 8$ matrix\n\nIII. $R$ is an upper triangular matrix" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=QR$ is a  QR factorization of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n$$\n{A}_{m\\times n}={Q}_{m\\times n}\\times {R}_{n\\times n},\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $Q$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix whose columns form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$\n\n- $R$ is an $n\\times n$ upper triangular matrix\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $Q$ must be an $8\\times 4$ matrix.\n\n- Statement II is false. $R$ should be a $4\\times 4$ matrix.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed $R$ must be an upper triangular matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432333 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133862 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38edbd80-2aef-5b3e-8dd9-de9f2fbeca9b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Triangular Factor of a QR Factorization Given the Orthogonal Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bd1aa0c-d1f1-5e19-8c7c-5cd02697146c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & -\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432291} {:answer/id #uuid "6759f5b6-d249-5365-af63-b9e06fadd6c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432295} {:answer/id #uuid "8130fec1-ce90-56ae-8304-b2a9297e4bd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432300} {:answer/id #uuid "965ab4c3-b74e-52d8-a355-600cbb8426c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432302} {:answer/id #uuid "b8e8a3d2-47ca-598e-8298-401f66b07744" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6759f5b6-d249-5365-af63-b9e06fadd6c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432295} :answer-field/id #uuid "34f73934-6b3e-5362-85fb-c078d25b34ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432320}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e1307433-5f5d-5677-8396-c15cc88708bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-95924" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Q$ shown above. Find the matrix $R$ such that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ has orthonormal columns, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{T}\\cdot A & ={Q}^{T}\\cdot QR \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & =({Q}^{T}\\,Q)R \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & ={I}_{2}R \\\\ R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that ${Q}^{T}Q={I}_{2}$ since the columns of $Q$ are orthonormal.\n\nNow, we calculate the matrix $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364585 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133862 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38edbd80-2aef-5b3e-8dd9-de9f2fbeca9b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Triangular Factor of a QR Factorization Given the Orthogonal Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f156e046-fa0d-5803-9d38-472b3668c9cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364587} {:answer/id #uuid "c218b0b0-ec5c-5f5e-b8ce-248a499d3534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364588} {:answer/id #uuid "69ec7d58-d9c0-5c9d-88fd-f9271de5e19b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364589} {:answer/id #uuid "df9b7366-5dbb-5188-8dca-12fd2020f705" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364590} {:answer/id #uuid "84264b94-1397-5e4e-a6e5-70dde4b0df3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df9b7366-5dbb-5188-8dca-12fd2020f705" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364590} :answer-field/id #uuid "68de1a49-3945-5406-b9a9-df87423f479c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364586}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "abcc772f-aef1-5bb8-8a2c-19affc4a7f6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-95931" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Q$ shown above. Find the matrix $R$ such that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ has orthonormal columns, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{T}\\cdot A & ={Q}^{T}\\cdot QR \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & =({Q}^{T}\\,Q)R \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & ={I}_{2}R \\\\ R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that ${Q}^{T}Q={I}_{2}$ since the columns of $Q$ are orthonormal.\n\nNow, we calculate the matrix $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432332 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133862 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38edbd80-2aef-5b3e-8dd9-de9f2fbeca9b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Triangular Factor of a QR Factorization Given the Orthogonal Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b22f01e-7a4d-5708-9e4c-f98dc75ccfb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432282} {:answer/id #uuid "45e9c0e6-c88e-53a1-9c43-cbdad38b1c50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432285} {:answer/id #uuid "5e041f60-e112-5cdc-8ca9-0a76aca85f33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432292} {:answer/id #uuid "a92bde20-f3b2-50bb-885a-b59500259f0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432306} {:answer/id #uuid "f7fb81ed-14f8-585c-8ccb-5a4e29de6310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45e9c0e6-c88e-53a1-9c43-cbdad38b1c50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432285} :answer-field/id #uuid "07052409-2507-5fcc-b1d5-dc865a195f43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c68cf512-7931-57e9-a96b-53e13136a3ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-95933" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Q$ shown above. Find the matrix $R$ such that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ has orthonormal columns, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{T}\\cdot A & ={Q}^{T}\\cdot QR \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & =({Q}^{T}\\,Q)R \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & ={I}_{2}R \\\\ R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that ${Q}^{T}Q={I}_{2}$ since the columns of $Q$ are orthonormal.\n\nNow, we calculate the matrix $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364592 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133862 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38edbd80-2aef-5b3e-8dd9-de9f2fbeca9b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Triangular Factor of a QR Factorization Given the Orthogonal Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "465339e6-0e6b-5ed2-9ec3-ea7c32d80d12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364594} {:answer/id #uuid "81627ba8-8d23-5e6c-b464-c7d40500c8c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364595} {:answer/id #uuid "f7b92ccf-3c22-5717-a199-7e9b189d529a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364596} {:answer/id #uuid "c8ba1bfd-3cf7-5350-80d8-d51f7211e2b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}3 & \\sqrt{5} \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364597} {:answer/id #uuid "24240a52-788b-5bf1-abe6-394b3adf3620" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364598}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24240a52-788b-5bf1-abe6-394b3adf3620" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364598} :answer-field/id #uuid "4aa403fe-1986-5cce-830e-fe0040a19a01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "273c61df-da34-56fd-9a6e-fc64509e7319" :question/math-academy-id "q-95934" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Q$ shown above. Find the matrix $R$ such that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ has orthonormal columns, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{T}\\cdot A & ={Q}^{T}\\cdot QR \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & =({Q}^{T}\\,Q)R \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & ={I}_{2}R \\\\ R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that ${Q}^{T}Q={I}_{2}$ since the columns of $Q$ are orthonormal.\n\nNow, we calculate the matrix $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380318 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133863 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ed5119c-5ce8-52dc-bd57-f9439ea199e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given a Matrix With Orthogonal Columns"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38c396ef-aa94-5fde-91e0-260c09178b68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380320} {:answer/id #uuid "4c5017ed-934c-561b-b1a1-2049ddab8c06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380321} {:answer/id #uuid "87b3f482-5de0-50ca-b081-5eb25a4c9360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380322} {:answer/id #uuid "c60e3738-5a9e-5b51-bb8b-21ac87f694a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380323} {:answer/id #uuid "958ede67-2778-5ce8-a57e-f1838011f997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c5017ed-934c-561b-b1a1-2049ddab8c06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380321} :answer-field/id #uuid "379ef46e-ea0e-5853-8ba7-b593f74e67fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "999c22e3-00be-5df4-8adf-f0fdfad70b35" :question/math-academy-id "q-95942" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{32}\\cdot {r}_{11}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{32}\\cdot {r}_{11}=-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 6=-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432330 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133863 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ed5119c-5ce8-52dc-bd57-f9439ea199e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given a Matrix With Orthogonal Columns"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4900a2e7-4ec1-5045-a690-39e44e6c099b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432286} {:answer/id #uuid "4ed122b1-a8ef-54bc-a477-491273558134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432288} {:answer/id #uuid "4edcb67b-0b50-5e1a-880f-c692e26158f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432289} {:answer/id #uuid "988c19f4-4b4b-5658-b6a9-5508c8ebffd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432303} {:answer/id #uuid "a409649e-ea5b-5f5d-997d-61959f000b9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432304}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "988c19f4-4b4b-5658-b6a9-5508c8ebffd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432303} :answer-field/id #uuid "039c8e12-c757-5b90-a48b-67f394056d16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432317}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "946d71c0-c7c2-59d9-aaa7-5ecb48201289" :question/math-academy-id "q-96015" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{21}\\cdot {r}_{11}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{14}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{14}\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{21}\\cdot {r}_{11}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364613 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133863 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ed5119c-5ce8-52dc-bd57-f9439ea199e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given a Matrix With Orthogonal Columns"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20f8fc6e-2098-5281-b2be-91340c5382ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364615} {:answer/id #uuid "fa9e3bfa-4b1c-5d94-adef-a29f1d34c643" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364616} {:answer/id #uuid "b2bad7d9-6fa9-551b-bbe8-3c2e9b0daab0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364617} {:answer/id #uuid "2247ba90-0af6-5144-9a63-c4d44090d3ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364618} {:answer/id #uuid "d0f7efcb-48ba-5cb1-951a-6612de87d27f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2bad7d9-6fa9-551b-bbe8-3c2e9b0daab0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364617} :answer-field/id #uuid "96755057-b7af-57c3-90a7-4ebdd3f1b964" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364614}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "75e8d7ef-26a5-51d9-ba2c-c43a53de12e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-96029" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{21}\\cdot {r}_{12}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{21}\\cdot {r}_{12}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}}\\cdot 0=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395107 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "19ae0632-68ac-5da9-8adf-cf71d5ecbc7c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the QR Factorization of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc8b3e92-0e0d-5d19-bb51-bfe62ab510a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395109} {:answer/id #uuid "e43f1009-aa53-5355-b6e2-6d08c550cf8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395110} {:answer/id #uuid "37c89cba-4da8-53a7-87c2-9de05914aaba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395111} {:answer/id #uuid "56b92eb0-336a-5fcf-8448-085005b2ce34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395112} {:answer/id #uuid "c551338c-b292-5e26-8b55-ecd82904ce69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395113}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c551338c-b292-5e26-8b55-ecd82904ce69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395113} :answer-field/id #uuid "eca9ce0b-73b4-529a-afb7-e8bec3879243" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1ae0f219-c74d-5218-aeb5-b468ecc59fef" :question/math-academy-id "q-96036" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{11}\\cdot {r}_{12}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-(-\\frac{9}{9})[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{11}\\cdot {r}_{12}=(\\frac{2}{3})\\cdot (-3)=-2.$"}]}
